# Endings: checked in this order on arrival day; the last one has no conditions.
# Each ending is one carrier clause (the fleet's hail vote) times one arrival clause
# (the storm vote: held the plan, waited it out in orbit, or no storm). Every ending
# closes on the first crate. `title` is the heading; see STORY_BIBLE.md.

card end_lights
type ending
cat ending
title Lights On
when flag signal_answered
when flag arrive_hold
text The fleet holds the plan it voted for, retargeted to the backup site. Cameras blind in the dust, radar sure of the ground; then Cargo 2, answered at last, sends a ranging tone and turns on every light it has. Seven ships land a careful three kilometres off. In the hold, the first crate says, in Sharpie: "You'll thank me." The first message off the surface goes to a loading bay at Starbase: "Landed. All eight."

card end_through
type ending
cat ending
title Through the Dust
when flag arrive_hold
text Retargeted to the backup site, as voted, the fleet goes down into the storm. The radar ranges on forty metres of standing steel; out of the brown, every eleven seconds, a light, still asking. Seven ships land three kilometres off. The first suit out carries the hail box and answers from close enough. In the hold, the first crate says, in Sharpie: "You'll thank me." The first message off the surface goes to a loading bay at Starbase: "Landed. All eight."

card end_long_orbit
type ending
cat ending
title The Long Orbit
when flag signal_answered
when flag arrive_wait
text Aerocapture, as voted, then four nights of ration math in a long orbit. Answered at last, Cargo 2 sends a little more of its manifest every pass, and the fleet reads it aloud like letters from home. When the sky clears, seven ships land in the prime zone and drive the rover out to it. In the hold, the first crate says, in Sharpie: "You'll thank me." The first message off the surface goes to a loading bay at Starbase: "Landed. All eight."

card end_patience
type ending
cat ending
title Patience
when flag arrive_wait
text Aerocapture, as voted, and four nights of half portions in orbit. On the third night the light skips a beat and seven people stop breathing; on the fourth it is back, faint in the dust, on time. When the sky clears the fleet lands, drives out, and answers the next knock from close enough. In the hold, the first crate says, in Sharpie: "You'll thank me." The first message off the surface goes to a loading bay at Starbase: "Landed. All eight."

card end_friend
type ending
cat ending
title Old Friend
when flag signal_answered
text Seven ships come down in clear air in the prime zone and drive out to a ship that waited two years for someone close enough to answer in time. Its lights hold steady now. On the drive out, Cargo 2 sends its whole manifest, slowly, like something that has been rehearsing. In the hold, the first crate says, in Sharpie: "You'll thank me." The first message off the surface goes to a loading bay at Starbase: "Landed. All eight."

card end_close
type ending
cat ending
title Close Enough
text Seven ships land in clear air. Then they drive Cargo 4's rover out to Cargo 2 with a radio retuned for the purpose and answer the next knock from two hundred metres: the first answer in two years to arrive while it's listening. The light stops blinking and stays on. In the hold, the first crate says, in Sharpie: "You'll thank me." The first message off the surface goes to a loading bay at Starbase: "Landed. All eight."
